<interrogationes unitas="praeconium">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record a Claude Code hook event in the session log?
    <responsum symbolum="praeconium_ex_hamo praeconium_scribere praeconium_via" via="tools/praeco.c">Parse
      the hook's stdin JSON with praeconium_ex_hamo (pass TMUX_PANE,
      TMUX and the time in ms), then praeconium_scribere to
      praeconium_via(piscina). This is exactly what 'praeco hamus'
      does. praeconium_ex_hamo returns FALSUM for malformed JSON or a
      non-object root, and then nothing should be written.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>where is the session event log, and how do I point a test at another one?
    <responsum symbolum="praeconium_via">praeconium_via
      returns $RHUBARB_COLLOQUIA/eventa.jsonl when that variable is
      set and non-empty, otherwise
      $HOME/.rhubarb/colloquia/eventa.jsonl (with HOME unset it falls
      back to ./.rhubarb/...). One file is shared by every worktree.
      Tests set RHUBARB_COLLOQUIA to a temporary directory;
      praeconium_scribere creates the directory if needed. Never set it
      to the empty string: the buffer is then sized for the empty value
      (plus LXIV) but the HOME path is written into it, which overflows
      once HOME is longer than 31 bytes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can several hooks append to the log at the same time without corrupting lines?
    <responsum symbolum="praeconium_scribere filum_appendere_firmiter">Yes:
      each event is one JSON line written by a single O_APPEND write()
      (then fsync) through filum_appendere_firmiter, so concurrent
      writers never interleave inside a line. The test forks four
      writers of 50 events and reads back all 200 lines intact. To keep
      lines short, nuntius is cut to 200 bytes, never inside a UTF-8
      character.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I follow the log incrementally, reading only new events?
    <responsum symbolum="praeconium_legere_ab praeconium_caudam_legere" via="lib/colloquia_visus.c">Keep
      an s64 byte position and call praeconium_legere_ab(via,
      &amp;positio, piscina) on each tick. Start at 0 to replay the
      whole file; to start from now, do what colloquia_visus does: set
      positio to the file size (fseek/ftell) FIRST, then seed your state
      once with praeconium_caudam_legere(via, octeti, piscina), which
      returns no position of its own (an event written in between is
      seen twice rather than lost). praeconium_legere_ab returns only complete
      lines and advances positio to the end of the last one, so a
      half-written line is read next time. If the file shrank below
      positio (deleted or rotated) it restarts from 0; if it is missing
      it returns an empty Xar and sets positio to 0.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do the genus and nuntius fields of a PraeconiumEventus hold?
    <responsum symbolum="PraeconiumEventus praeconium_ex_hamo" via="lib/praeconium.worklog.md">They
      depend on hook_event_name: Notification gives notification_type
      and message; PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostToolUseFailure and
      PermissionRequest give tool_name and the first non-empty of
      tool_input description, command, file_path, pattern, url;
      UserPromptSubmit puts the prompt in nuntius; Stop and SubagentStop
      the last_assistant_message; StopFailure the error; SessionStart
      sets genus to trigger (or the older source); SessionEnd to
      reason. servitor is only the socket part of TMUX (before the
      first comma).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep the chorda fields of an event after resetting the piscina I read it with?
    <responsum symbolum="praeconium_ex_hamo praeconium_caudam_legere praeconium_legere_ab chorda_ut_cstr">No.
      Every chorda in a PraeconiumEventus points into JSON values
      allocated in the piscina you passed; resetting it (colloquia_visus
      calls piscina_vacare on its scratch piscina every tick) leaves
      them dangling. Copy what you keep. They are chorda, not
      NUL-terminated: use chorda_ut_cstr before printf or strcmp.
      Missing fields are empty chorda (datum NIHIL).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
